[Music]  meanwhile an ottawa judge has granted a  10-day injunction to silence the  incessant honking from parked trucks on  the streets the decision comes after a  multi-million dollar class action  lawsuit was filed by a group of ottawa  residents they argue the prolonged noise  is causing permanent harm  so what does this decision truly mean  and how will this injunction be enforced  joining us now is litigation lawyer fred  schuman fred we appreciate you joining  us a bit of a complex issue in many in  many respects but let's begin with the  basics the injunction came out of a  class action lawsuit what exactly does  this mean  so an injunction is just a court order  that tells somebody they have to do  something or that they can't do  something  it can be enforced by the police  arresting somebody for contempt of court  and it lasts in this case for ten days  so uh that's what the court has ordered  the court is essentially ordered in this  case that you can't use a horn in  downtown ottawa unless you're a fire  truck basically all right so there are a  lot of trucks uh potentially a lot of  protesters as well so let's talk about  is this even enforceable given the  situation  well i i mean  yeah it's it's enforceable there may be  difficulties with enforcing it i mean  remember it's an anti-honk injunction  it's not an anti-protest injunction and  i think as most classroom teachers know  if somebody makes a noise and you're not  watching them at that very moment it can  be difficult to  to tell who made the noise  so there may be practical difficulties  with actually identifying somebody and  convicting them of contempt of court but  the order authorizes the police to  arrest people uh if they have reasonable  grounds to believe that they've  contravened the order by honking so that  may be something easier for the police  to  to manage and form those grounds um but  the latest reports i've seen have  suggested that the injunction has worked  not because it's being enforced but  because people simply seem to be  complying with it and and not honking  so i mean actual enforcement isn't  always necessary sometimes people just  do what the courts ordered them to do  voluntarily what's interesting to break  this down is this is a civil suit this  isn't a criminal court that is deciding  this so you kind of wonder where this  could go i mean the honking is one thing  but even beyond this this is a class  action lawsuit  9.8 million dollars is what they were  seeking in terms of damages um so the  few layers to this but how long could  this process take you know from the from  the plaintiff's point of view  well that depends i think if the  plaintiffs are really interested in in  pushing this all the way to a trial or  something like that i mean class action  lawsuits can take years to actually come  to that final stage but i'm not sure  that the plaintiffs are really  in this for the long haul in that sense  i mean if i were representing the  plaintiffs i think i would have been  interested in starting a class-action  lawsuit not because um i necessarily  want to go to trial not because it's  necessary to get the injunction you  don't have to have a class action  lawsuit to get an injunction like this  but to to make the threat that was made  a few days ago of um  large money damages against the  protesters so a few days ago there was  this threat by the plaintiff's lawyers i  don't mean threat in a to criticize them  but they were simply saying if you stay  here and you continue these activities  you are going to be a defendant in this  lawsuit and you're going to potentially  be on the hook  for um a very large amount of money  damages because the damages are being  suffered by everybody in the area and  they were offering to release the  protesters legally from that lawsuit if  they left and so the class action  tool was useful in making that threat  credibly and offering people a release  if they left but it is at the end of the  day just a reprieve so this injunction  in effect for 10 days do we have a sense  what happens after that  yeah so in 10 day where in nine days the  plaintiff's lawyers will go back to  court back to the judge who who ordered  this injunction and remember there were  some there was one lawyer there named  keith wilson who represented some of the  protesters uh perhaps the organizers i  think so he was there he opposed the  injunction yesterday  and um they'll be back before the judge  in nine days  to seek and the plaintiffs will be  seeking an extension seeking to continue  that injunction and they need to do that  because  the judge wasn't satisfied that all of  the protesters had notice of the motion  for an injunction so they didn't all  know that this was coming to court they  didn't all have the opportunity to be  there yesterday and tell their side of  the story so the judge is only allowed  to make an order that lasts for 10 days  so but he's already booked the return  date which is coming back to court the  plaintiffs will say we want another  10 days or you know we've done all these  things in the interim we've um  broadcast the order on the on the tv and  amplified it uh and you know you should  grant an injunction now for a longer  period of time well this could take a  while in a number of court cases we  appreciate this fred schumann litigation  lawyer we appreciate your time sir  thanks very much for having

